% A unit's start reading what another has not made yet, and two units of
% one name (docs/plans/done/plan_bug_analyses.md, A10): mini-analyze -bugs,
% over the call graph of points_to/ (target) and the facts of
% Closure_facts and mini-analyze (FACTS.md):
%   global_def(G, I)       the global G is made by the unit start I
%                          (U:init)
%   var_in(G, F)           F names G: reads it, calls it
%   starts_before(I, J)    the unit start I runs before J (mini-analyze
%                          -start's order, as mini-ml -start's; else
%                          only the library's units before the files')
%   unit_start(U, I)       I is the unit U's start
%   unit_file(U, F)        a file of the unit U's name in a directory
%                          whose units name U
% and says:
%   early(I, G, F, J)      I's start, in F, reads G, which the start J
%                          makes, J running after I: G not made yet
%   twice(U, F1, F2)       two files of U's name, one program: which one
%                          is linked is the build's choice
%
%     let field = Bignum.of_int (1 lsl Bignum.base_bits)   (70b6b2bf^,
%                                                         Poly1305.ml)
%
% Poly1305 linked before Bignum: base_bits was 0 when Poly1305's toplevel
% read it, and the program did not start (a loop that never ended). And
% mini-doom's Collide beside lib_physics's, both libraries unwrapped:
% dune linked one silently, the walks a segmentation fault (6264d271).
%
% design:
% OCaml's linker refuses the first (a unit given before those it names:
% "Reference to undefined global"), and dune the second only in one
% library; ix's mini-ld runs the starts in -start's order, which the
% mkfiles write (mkprog: STD, COMMONS, the libraries' UNITS, SRC), and
% checks neither. What a start runs is its code and the functions it
% calls, through the call graph; a function made there and called later
% is not (its body another function). A start with no order against
% another (two of the files, without -start) is not compared.
%
% others:
% C++'s "static initialization order fiasco" is the same bug across
% translation units (its cure a function's local static, made at its
% first use); Java runs a class's initializer at its first use (JLS
% 12.4), so the order is the uses', not the link's; OCaml's -linkall and
% its check of the order at link time are what mini-ld has not.

runs_at(I, I) :- init(I).
runs_at(G, I) :- runs_at(F, I), in_function(C, F), target(C, G).

% (a function of a unit that starts later runs at I's start only as a
% value it gave, registered by its own start, later: what the call graph
% does not order, Oberon's tasks; one I's code calls is named there)
late_code(F, I) :- alloc_in(F, U), unit_start(U, J), starts_before(I, J).
early(I, G, F, J) :-
  runs_at(F, I), \+ late_code(F, I), var_in(G, F), global_def(G, J), starts_before(I, J).

twice(U, F1, F2) :- unit_file(U, F1), unit_file(U, F2), F1 \= F2.
